WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.460
The Taliban over running everything

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.453
and owning the whole
country is highly unlikely.

00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:08.000
The Taliban have taken Afghanistan.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Afghans are thronging to Kabul's Airport.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.070
They took over the presidential palace.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.810
Afghanistan's president fled the country.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.200
You don't get to lose a war

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.940
and expect the result
to look like you won.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.160
That's how historian Stephen
Wertheim sums up the violent

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.930
and chaotic withdrawal
of United States forces

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.180
and personnel from Afghanistan.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.990
A senior fellow in the
American Statecraft Program

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.870
at the Carnegie Endowment
for International Peace,

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.400
Wertheim is the author
of "Tomorrow, the World,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.880
the Birth of US Global Supremacy."

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.960
A study of the intellectual origins

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.870
of America's interventionist
foreign policy.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.550
He talked with reason about
how the wars in Afghanistan

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.277
and Iraq were doomed
from their earliest days.

00:00:49.277 --> 00:00:53.313
And what policy makers should
be focused on as we approach

00:00:53.313 --> 00:00:56.855
the 20th anniversary of the 9/11 attacks.

00:00:56.855 --> 00:00:59.281
Stephen Wertheim, thanks
for talking to Reason.

00:00:59.281 --> 00:01:00.563
It's a pleasure to be with you.

00:01:00.563 --> 00:01:03.197
So let's talk about Afghanistan.

00:01:03.197 --> 00:01:05.607
You wrote recently as the withdrawal

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.130
was just being kind of everywhere.

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.830
You said, you know, we lost the war.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.120
We shouldn't be surprised
that we don't really get

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.370
to set the terms of how it ends.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:17.350
What did you mean by that?

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:21.150
I meant that somehow a lot of people who

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:26.150
had favored transforming the
entire country of Afghanistan

00:01:26.785 --> 00:01:31.785
by force and who failed to
achieve that over two decades.

00:01:31.987 --> 00:01:34.810
Somehow they apparently
thought that having failed

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.690
to achieve that, we would
also set the terms of the exit

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.860
so that it looked like a happy scene

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.770
or something at the end.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.770
Well, unless you think that
the Taliban are magnanimous

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.360
and their takeover of the
country is going to go smoothly.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.819
And have no adverse
humanitarian consequences.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.100
And I don't think these
people do actually,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.630
I don't think anyone believes that.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:09.630
It was bound to be a
terrible, a terrible tragedy.

00:02:09.723 --> 00:02:14.723
So I think it's certainly
legitimate to question whether

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.250
the Biden administration
could have conducted

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.420
the withdrawal in a different
way, in a better way?

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.420
It's possible that over the
summer, it might have foreseen

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.230
that the government in Kabul
would have fallen quickly

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.190
and therefore would
have acted more quickly

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:41.060
to evacuate Americans and
Afghans from the country.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.010
But it is far from obvious that

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.260
that is a reasonable expectation.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.526
Given that most analysts
who I've heard from

00:02:48.526 --> 00:02:52.970
and spoken with thought
that it would take quite

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.740
a bit longer for the government to fall.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.790
Or that potentially there
might be some ability to have

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:03.191
a sharing agreement between
the Kabul government

00:03:03.191 --> 00:03:05.171
and the Taliban.

00:03:05.171 --> 00:03:09.730
In fact, I heard very few
people prior to the exact moment

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.100
when Kabul fell, predict
what actually happened

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.840
in the way that it happened.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.740
So what I'm afraid we've seen
is a kind of repetition of

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.310
the hubris that got us into the war

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.770
and sustained the war over two decades.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.480
Well, I wanna get to a
question about the US,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.650
and I hesitate to call it
the Biden administration.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.750
I mean, he's in charge of
it and he has to own it

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.470
but this was, having to,
he's the fourth president

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:39.700
to oversee this.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.230
But what about the collapse
of the Afghan government

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.840
that we put in and the
army and all of that,

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.804
the massive and immediate
collapse of that,

00:03:49.804 --> 00:03:54.804
does that, is that an
obvious kind of extension,

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.929
a logical conclusion to the folly of

00:03:56.929 --> 00:03:58.790
being there in the first place?

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:03.790
It certainly proves the
point that it was a folly.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.583
I mean, it raises the question.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.060
Not only did we clearly not succeed

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.460
in the nation building mission,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.721
it raises the question of
whether the past decade

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:20.153
of US involvement in the
war actually built anything?

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.830
To see the government that was supposed

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:28.340
to become selfreliant fold so quickly.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:32.610
Now again, I don't wanna say
that it was inevitable from

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.060
the moment Biden announced the withdrawal

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.540
of ground troops back
in April of this year,

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.320
that that government would
collapse in the way that it did

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.470
with the speed that it did, et cetera.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.925
But it certainly doesn't make
me more inclined to think.

00:04:47.925 --> 00:04:52.925
Well, if only the United
States had stayed longer,

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:57.530
fought harder, this could have
been a successful mission.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.271
What could the Biden administration
have done differently?

